   "": {
    "actClause": "AN ACT to amend the education law, in relation to creating a state-wide\nliteracy education advocacy program; and to study the viability of the\nNewburgh armory unity center literacy education advocacy partnership",
    "basePrintNo": "S7995",
    "basePrintNoStr": "S7995-2019",
    "coSponsors": {
     "items": [],
     "size": 0
    },
    "fullText": "",
    "fullTextFormats": [],
    "fullTextHtml": "",
    "fullTextTemplate": "",
    "lawCode": "Amd §305, Ed L",
    "lawSection": "Education Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S7995\n\nSPONSOR: SKOUFIS\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the education law, in relation to creating a state-wide\nliteracy education advocacy program; and to study the viability of the\nNewburgh armory unity center literacy education advocacy partnership\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo study the effectiveness of the literacy education program at the\nNewburgh Armory Unity Center and evaluate whether it may serve as a\nmodel for other programs to increase childhood literacy rates.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection 1: The Commissioner of Education is directed to conduct a study\non the Newburgh Armory Unity Center Literacy Education Advocacy Partner-\nship in order to determine the success and effectiveness of the program\nin achieving its goals, with the intent of understanding effective prac-\ntices that support students being able to read at grade three and score\nat level three and four on the third grade English language arts state\nassessment.\n\nSection 2: Within one year of the enactment of this bill, the Commis-\nsioner will report the findings, conclusions, and recommendations of the\nstudy to the governor, temporary president of the Senate and speaker of\nthe Assembly.\n\nSection 3: Section 305 of the Education Law is amended by adding a new\nsubdivision 57 that requires the Commissioner of Education to develop a\nliteracy education advocacy program for elementary school-aged children.\nThe program would be optional for school districts across the state and\nwould utilize the study performed on the Newburgh Armory Unity Center\nLiteracy Education Advocacy Partnership.\n\nSection 4: Effective date.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nEnsuring that children obtain the literacy skills they need to succeed\nin life is critical. While local schools are the primary place that\nstudents learn to read and write, extracurricular programs such as the\nNewburgh Armory Unity Center Literacy Education Advocacy Partnership\n(LEAP) can be a vital tool to provide students with targeted and effec-\ntive literacy education. This innovative program improves the literacy\nperformance of elementary students in the school district by providing\npersonalized, direct, one-on-one literacy instruction to identified\nstudents and integrates family and community involvement through\nrequired, defined tasks and responsibilities connected to specified\ngoals for each child. A 2016 study by the Benjamin Center at SUNY New\nPaltz found measurable increases in literacy scores for students\ninvolved in the LEAP program; if a study by the Department of Education\nfinds similar positive results, the LEAP program could serve as a state-\nwide model for other districts looking to provide a comprehensive liter-\nacy education to high-needs students.\n\n \nLEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\nNew bill\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nTo be determined, but minimal.\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately; provided, however, section three\nof this act shall take effect on the same date as the completion of the\nstudy established under section one of this act provided that the\ncommissioner of education shall notify the legislative bill drafting\ncommission upon the completion of such study in order that the commis-\nsion may maintain an accurate and timely effective data base of the\nofficial text of the laws of the state of New York in furtherance of\neffecting the provisions of section 44 of the legislative law and\nsection 70-b of the public officers law.\n",
